# COPYRIGHT (C) PANUA TECHNOLOGIES, 2023

Panua PARDISO is the most recent parallel implementation of the 
well-known sparse linear PARDISO software. It integrates recent advances
on high-performance parallel linear solver technology. The package PARDISO 
is a thread-safe, high-performance, robust, memory  efficient and easy to 
use software for solving large sparse symmetric and unsymmetric linear 
systems of equations on shared-memory,  distributed-memory multiprocessors 
and GPUs.


****** CONTENT ******

This package includes the following directories:

lib    : shared library containing the Panua Pardiso solver
include: include files
bin    : binaries (license verification)
test   : test instances

****** LICENSE ******

In order to use Panua PARDISO you need to obtain a license key at 
www.panua.ch and put it into a file called panua.lic.  This file may 
contain more than one license key.  Put this file into your home 
directory, or set the environment variable PANUA_LIC_PATH to the directory 
that contains panua.lic.

To verify that your license key is set up properly, run the 
"check_license" binary in the bin directory of the distribution.  Make 
sure that it reports that a valid Ipopt license could be found.

In order to obtain a license, you need to generate a "fingerprint".  You 
do this by running the "get_fingerprint" executable in the bin 
subdirectory on the machine where you want to use Panua PARDISO.  For 
single-user licenses, you need to be logged in as the user that will use 
Panua PARDISO.  (Note that when you run your code on a computing cluster 
that uses a scheduling system, your code will be run on different physical 
machines that all have different fingerprints.)

****** RUNNING TESTS ******

To see if the software works on your system, run "make test" in the test 
subdirectory of the distribution.  This compiles some simple C++, C, and 
FORTRAN examples, and runs PARDISO in parallel for up  to 4 cores, and up 
to 2 cores for academic trial licenses.

By default, the GNU compilers are chosen.  But any other compiler that is 
compatible with the GNU compilers (such as the Intel compilers) should 
work as well.

The Makefile might be a good start for writing your own Makefile to link 
with PARDISO.  (Ignore the part that runs the test suite.)

****** DOCUMENTATION ******

You can refer to the corresponding  documentation on PANUA:

  https://panua.ch/static/manual/manual.pdf

Once additional features become available, further documentation will be 
available on the Panua website.

****** BINARY COMPATIBILITY ******

If you already have your code integrated with PARDISO, via the shared 
library option, you can simply replace your previous shared library with 
the one contained in this package.

****** UPDATES ******

We release updated versions of the library from time to time.  Unless 
there is a release of a new feature, you can obtain the most recent 
version at www.panua.ch at any time.

****** SUPPORT ******

This software is provided as is and no warranty is granted.  We make every 
attempt to provide software that links and runs properly, but we cannot 
provide individualized support and we make no guarantees on the 
performance of the algorithms.  You may report suspected bugs at 
www.panua.ch and we will attempt to fix actual errors in a reasonable 
response time.  Note, however, that improper use or call of the library is 
the cause of the issue in most of the cases.

